    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery — Cloud Service Discovery / current-context check", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 10:02:14 CEST, Wazuh's GCP module ingested a Cloud Audit Log (Admin Activity) entry showing a gcloud auth list / google.iam.credentials.v1.IAMCredentials call. The caller identity is the service account ci-deploy@corp-prod-987654.iam.gserviceaccount.com inside project corp-prod-987654, and the callerIp recorded in the audit log is 194.165.16.89 with a userAgent of 'google-cloud-sdk/453.0.0 Python/3.11 Linux'. Wazuh fired a rule whose rule.groups contains 'gcp' and 'gcloud' and rule.description references the IAM credentials / auth-context check. A CI/CD service account establishing interactive gcloud context from a non-CI public IP is the opening move of stolen-key abuse.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'gcp' + 'gcloud' and rule.description matched the IAM credentials auth-context call, with the audit log callerIp 194.165.16.89 — an IP that has never been associated with the CI/CD pipeline ranges for ci-deploy@.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:14+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery — Cloud Infrastructure Discovery / fleet enumeration", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "At 10:04:30 CEST (~2 minutes later), the GCP module ingested an Admin Activity log entry for compute.instances.list across project corp-prod-987654, same service account ci-deploy@, same callerIp 194.165.16.89. Wazuh fired rule 92965 ('GCP Admin Activity: compute.instances.get / list from unexpected account') mapped to T1651. The service account enumerated the entire Compute Engine fleet — the attacker is building a target list of VMs before pivoting.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92965 fired with rule.groups containing 'gcp' and rule.description referencing compute.instances enumeration, callerIp 194.165.16.89, principal ci-deploy@corp-prod-987654.iam.gserviceaccount.com — the same key seen establishing context in step 1.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:04:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Lateral Movement — Cloud Administration Command / SSH key injection fan-out", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "From 10:06:10 to 10:14:09 CEST, the GCP module ingested a burst of Admin Activity log entries for oslogin.sshPublicKeys.create and compute.instances.get targeting four VMs in zone europe-west1-b — vm-app-01, vm-app-02, vm-db-01 and vm-bastion — all from service account ci-deploy@ and callerIp 194.165.16.89. Each oslogin.sshPublicKeys.create event represents the attacker injecting an SSH public key into a VM's OS Login profile via the GCP API, which grants direct SSH access without touching the instance's own auth. Wazuh fired rule 92964 ('GCP Compute SSH via API from unexpected account') mapped to T1651 for each VM. This is the actionable lateral-movement step: API-driven SSH access fanned out across the production fleet, including the bastion host.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92964 fired repeatedly with rule.description referencing oslogin.sshPublicKeys.create / compute SSH via API, rule.groups containing 'gcp' and 'gcloud', callerIp 194.165.16.89, the same principal ci-deploy@ — across four distinct VM names in europe-west1-b within an 8-minute window. The auth-list -> instances-list -> ssh-fan-out sequence from one stolen key is the canonical Cloud Administration Command lateral-movement signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:06:10+0200"}
